# Sound the Death Knell for the Caste System
Tamil title: ஜாதி முறைக்குச் சாவுமணி

Merely talking of the ideal of seeing a casteless society, spending our days discussing the arguments and counterarguments concerning it, pouring out quotations, taking the same source and having both sides interpret it to find support for their respective views, and then leaving this accursed caste system to our descendants as an unresolved case, an uncut knot and an uncured disease would be neither courageous nor intelligent.

We sometimes hear this counsel: “The caste system is certainly cruel and must certainly be removed. Intellectuals understand its cruelty well, and the people have begun to recognize it too. Henceforth the work of gradually abolishing this cruelty will take place. We must not act angrily or hastily, because this is a poisonous tree that has somehow taken root since ancient times. This cruelty can disappear only very slowly. Therefore one must neither speak angrily and passionately about caste distinctions nor denounce the system.” This advice is not merely insipid; it is also a device for preventing the public from recognizing the path of truth. Once something is known to be cruel, the duty of people possessing both intelligence and courage is to work for its abolition. When an incompatible marriage has ruined the life of a woman as precious as a golden vine, consoling her by saying, “Whom has fate spared, my child?” is not merely a sign of affection for her; it is also a device for denying her the right to remarry.

While a rich man's grandson strolls upon the upper storey and the poor man writhes below, the poor man looks up and asks himself: “Why should he live like that? Why am I tormented like this? Have I no right to a good life? Why should steps not be taken to secure that right?” He reaches a decision and begins opposing the system that has lowered him and raised another. If someone then philosophizes, “Brother, what deed have you dared to undertake? He lives that way today as the reward for merits earned in a former birth; you now experience the fruit of your former karma,” that person is working to crush the poor man's revolutionary power and protect the position of the upper class, not expounding a spiritual truth.

It is the same when people say with a mingled smile and sigh: “Something arose long ago; we do not understand its subtleties. At one time the caste system may even have produced benefits. It is unnecessary today, but can such a system be demolished suddenly?” Unable to prove before learned people that their side is right, they evade the issue with excuses, reasoning that however long they can postpone the matter is so much profit and asking why they should surrender that profit. This is nothing but a selfish stratagem. Why keep inventing philosophy for an improper scheme?

On seeing a cobra spread its hood, one might say: “If only this beautiful snake had no venomous fangs, would not every home keep one as a plaything? People like to raise dogs, cats, parrots and doves; would they not instead raise snakes, glossy and smooth, curving as they dance, breathing like music issuing from a plucked tambura, and lovely to behold? Would they not make a gold-framed glass case, place the snake inside it and enjoy the sight?” Likewise, saying that caste distinction appears fit for abolition only because it creates jealousy and quarrels is a deceitful attempt to gild and prolong a wicked system.

Many in this country practise such deceit. This is indeed a land of gold, but it is a land caught and ruined for ages by impostors. By offering attractive interpretations of poisonous doctrines, those impostors have penned workers like cattle. This is a city of sin, a country containing many innocents whose minds have been so robbed by religious intoxication that they worship cow dung as Maheswara.

It is a country filled with ruinous customs that make the ignorant believe that washing a Brahmin guru's feet and drinking that water is a potion granting admission to heaven. Like a hen dancing before a fox as though that were its duty, multitudes perish believing the person who undermines them to be God's cherished child and the person who subjugates them to be the people's protector.

At a time when radicals eagerly wonder whether such a country might at least find a new path with the aid of the light of knowledge now spreading through the world, and when they labour toward that end, the people who apply this gilding use deceit to corrupt both sound thinkers and our countrymen who have already trusted and been betrayed.

A woman of loose conduct asks her companion, “Who is this gentleman of such elegance?” Learning that he is a prince with a milk-soft face and small blue eyes, she calls to him, “Handsome one! Man of charming virtues!” and casts her allure upon him. Refusing to become a victim of her desire, resolved not to surrender his heart to that deceitful woman, he says: “Woman blinded by lust, be gone at once! Otherwise prepare yourself for this dagger.” Seeing firmness in his eye and sharpness in his dagger, she recoils. Enraged that her arts have failed, she abuses him: “Demon who insults a woman! Rustic who knows nothing of civilized refinement! Is it becoming of a man to revile me when I come to offer you a fine feast?”

In the same manner, courageous people use the light of knowledge spreading across the world to seek a new path and refuse to fall into pits dug by poison-minded flatterers along the way. Antiquity, which holds within itself the cruelties of caste distinction and superstition, attempts to intoxicate them, excite desire and enslave them with affectionate words: “Was I not created especially for you by the noblest people? Look at my beauty! Look at my youth! Destiny declares that you are for me and I for you. Do not leave me.”

When the brave person replies firmly, “I know your tricks; I will not fall into your net,” and proceeds along the straight path, antiquity tries at least to ease its burning resentment by abusing the prey that refuses it: “You who reject antiquity are a sinner! You who criticize ancient customs are a criminal! You who condemn the way of life esteemed since your ancestors' time are a base man, an atheist!” A person who trembles at this abuse cannot perform the good work that will bring well-being to the country. The adventuress who tries to capture a young man with dalliance will curse him when she fails; the duty of the resolute is to understand that her dalliance is more deadly than her curse.

For more than twenty years in Tamil Nadu, a body without publicity strength has made that very duty its life's ideal and laboured at it. The intellectual force called the Self-Respecters alone has refused to fear this curse and has continued attacking and demolishing the cruel effects of antiquity. Those who do not understand the purpose of these fighters for justice accuse them of using harsh words, failing to understand circumstances and possessing corrupt minds.

The truth is that these fighters possess no beggar's mentality, cowardice or fraud. They have no fear. They are not wicked people who hide treachery in their hearts as poison hides in an anthill while attaching courteous words to their lips. There is no trickery in their thought, and therefore no subtle net in their speech. Their minds contain no confusion, and so they have no habit of stringing together ambiguous terms into a garland of flattery.

They undertake nothing in expectation of an immediate gain or a reward for personal advancement. Their sole aim in life is to find the means by which the self-respect of a great community will not be plundered by a few selfish people. Assault therefore does not make them bow, deceit does not bend them, threats do not bewilder them, and they are neither caught in a grip nor trapped in a net. They do not lower their heads. Enduring showers of abuse, unconcerned whether victory can be won or cannot be won, they fearlessly follow the path of righteous struggle with only their objective in mind.

They are not unaware that some others have received rewards and acquired prestige. But a man who twists a topknot onto a monkey, dresses it in a coloured coat and ties bells to its tail to make it perform does so for his livelihood, not out of love for the monkey. In the same way, they understand that the status bestowed upon petty people who abandoned the noble task of engaging in righteous struggle and transforming afflicted people into courageous human beings is merely an ornamental coat given to a slave, not a symbol of rights. They resolve not to follow that path. Though the road is filled with stones and thorns, clear-minded people decide that they must travel only the path of duty, and so they do.

The opposition facing them is powerful and assumes many forms. Nevertheless, the assault of that intellectual force has not failed to bring success. Seeing the degraded present condition of the country's people and investigating its cause, this force discovered that the people remain undeveloped in a developing world and that antiquity prevents their growth. It resolved that the cruelty by which the people of a resource-rich country languish in poverty as though destitute and remain submerged in ignorance must be ended. It girded itself and began its work.

Her limbs are golden, but a white film covers her eyes; her entire face is scarred. On seeing that pitiable woman, would one not think how beautiful she must have been when her eyes were sound and her face unscarred? Are we logs of wood? We are people with hearts. Can we see her and refrain from saying, “She is beautiful, but smallpox has disfigured her”? On seeing the horror the disease has inflicted upon her, could anyone forget her former beauty and exclaim instead: “Ah, what marvellous power smallpox possesses! What extraordinary strength! See its victory. She was a golden statue of the highest purity. Smallpox has carved her face as though with a tiny chisel. She herself is proof of the disease's extraordinary victory!”

A lovely infant has arms and legs like golden cylinders, eyes like diamond lamps, and a body of fresh gold. It is at the age of baby talk, yet cannot speak. Convulsions struck while it still lay in the cradle, and the child lost the power of speech. Would anyone who saw such a child sing a hymn of praise to the convulsive illness?

An entire people lies ruined like a collapsed wall, a destroyed grove and a disordered roof. Is it possible not to wonder how far that people might have advanced if some hostile storm had not once struck them?

Likewise, once it becomes clear what has caused the present degradation of a resourceful people who once lived prosperously, can we refrain from stating that cause openly or seeking a remedy? While seeking one, can we fail to resolve not to submit merely because hostility arises? Can such people be subdued or suppressed by calling their resolve arrogance and their intellect atheism?

A priest with a bent back enters carrying a staff in one hand and a bundle of darbha grass in the other, with a tattered almanac under his arm. Inside, several people sob and wipe their eyes. A patient's life hangs by a thread; phlegm chokes him, and he no longer feels flies and ants biting. His son or brother sits broken-hearted. The figure who has entered does not ask what the illness is or what medicine has been given. Instead he says: “Have you seen the difficulty that this soul experiences in leaving the body? Alas! A great sin, a grave fault! Unless a cow is donated, the life cannot depart peacefully.” Once this is said, the task is accomplished, and he keeps urging them to attend to the necessary rite.

One may suppose that such a meaningless scene could not occur in the homes of an intellectual and politically astute man such as Dr Subbarayan, who can find a ministerial place under many kinds of political arrangement. Yet do not more than ninety people in every hundred regard this spectacle as not merely perfectly ordinary but prescribed by scripture? Is this not an event that takes place in the country? Surely sound thinkers would wish to abolish such a meaningless custom entirely.

How is it to be abolished? Even the stone-hearted would not say that everyone must first qualify as a barrister, attain maturity of mind and abandon it independently without anyone else's prompting. Did our national leaders preach that Swaraj should come to us only after every man and woman in Britain had understood the nature of righteousness and recognized that keeping India enslaved was unrighteous? We fought for it!

In precisely the same way, we must oppose, fight and abolish the custom by which orthodox ritualists enter the homes of mentally confused people like officials collecting a death tax. What does opposing and fighting mean? It means exposing the obscenity and harm of that custom. Unless it is exposed, how can the minds of people long involved in it change?

But when it is exposed, if they begin crying, “Ah! A Brahmin-hater!” summon the government to their aid, and complain, “This criminal insults my almanac and my hereditary custom; seize the fellow and throw him into prison,” how can the work of reconstructing the country proceed? Do this country's intellectuals accord a question possessing such clarity and justice the importance it deserves?

Why should the youth of this country not unite specifically to sound the death knell for this cruel caste order? Why should they not begin an agitation devoted to abolishing caste? Why should they not hold a separate conference to direct the people's attention to this question and impress their duty upon those who govern?

Published in *Dravida Nadu*, 25.48.

---NOTES---
The separate title crop was omitted from the OCR Markdown. Its printed title and opening two lines were restored from `001-be1f4de7ac.png`; they join directly to the beginning of `001-57d6278acb.png`.

The source prints the publication reference as “25.48,” which is retained without silently expanding or guessing its date.

Several source readings remain visibly affected by OCR, including “இரு காட்சிகளும்” (likely “இரு கட்சிகளும்”), “சுபரால்யம்” (in context, Swaraj), and “மேனி பசும்பொன் ... வைர மஎரிகள்.” The Tamil source block is preserved as corrected, while the English follows the evident contextual meaning.
